{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.01","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.monoid","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-operation-internal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"operation ⋆:M×M→M","editorial_reason":"A monoid explicitly carries an internally closed binary operation M×M→M, so the internal binary operation is a foundational part of the structure before the associativity and identity axioms are imposed.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMonoid","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOperation/Binary_Operation","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.02","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.binary-operation-general","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"S×T","editorial_reason":"The source signature ⋆:S×T→U uses the binary Cartesian product as the domain of ordered pairs; this prerequisite holds without requiring closure on either input set.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOperation/Binary_Operation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.03","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.binary-operation-internal","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"S×S","editorial_reason":"An internal operation has a Cartesian product S×S as domain, hence requires ordered pairs of elements of S; the same-set codomain restriction is an additional condition.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOperation/Binary_Operation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.04","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-typed","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"S×T","editorial_reason":"The typed relation (S,T,R) assumes that R is contained in the Cartesian product S×T. The component sets and relation graph remain distinct data.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.05","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-subset","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"S×T","editorial_reason":"The graph-only convention identifies a binary relation with a subset of S×T, requiring the independently defined ordered-pair Cartesian product.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.06","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.group","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.monoid","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"binary operation","editorial_reason":"The group axioms include associative multiplication and an identity element, which together make the underlying operation a monoid; requiring inverses is the additional group condition. This uses the general monoid meaning, not commutativity.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AGroup","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMonoid","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.07","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.group","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-operation-internal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"binary operation","editorial_reason":"The group operation is explicitly typed G×G→G, hence it is an internally closed binary operation. This prerequisite remains valid independent of identity and inverse axioms.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AGroup","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOperation/Binary_Operation","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.08","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.associative-ring-identity-optional","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-operation-internal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"operations + and ·","editorial_reason":"A ring has two internally closed binary operations, additive and multiplicative, each mapping R×R to R. This holds for rings without multiplicative identity and does not make their multiplication a monoid.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARing_%28Abstract_Algebra%29","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOperation/Binary_Operation","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.09","from_formulation":"form.709d3d2f82950c5b.preordered-set","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-subset","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"binary relation","editorial_reason":"A preorder is specifically a reflexive and transitive binary relation on a set, represented by a subset of I×I. Its reflexivity and transitivity are additional properties of that graph relation.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002Z","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.10","from_formulation":"form.0c2b2ef0c7e5c91b.multiplicative-monoid-character","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.monoid","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"monoid G","editorial_reason":"A field-valued multiplicative character is explicitly a monoid homomorphism from G, so its domain must satisfy the associative-operation and identity axioms. Homomorphism itself remains independently unsourced.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CKK","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMonoid","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch07.11","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.small-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"Hom_C(y,z)×Hom_C(x,y)","editorial_reason":"The source composition maps take ordered pairs of composable arrows from a Cartesian product of hom sets. The Cartesian product is foundational but does not substitute for category identity or associativity axioms.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_mathematical_scope_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
